{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show an upswing, the model underpredicts the level over the next ~10–20 days. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–20).",
    "Trend/lag error: the forecast path reacts too slowly to strong upward moves (it stays near ~68–70 while realized moves into ~71–73). Add a trend-following term proportional to recent momentum (e.g., last 5–7 day slope) for horizons ~5–20 days.",
    "Peak underestimation: during rally phases the model misses the amplitude of local highs (notably around day ~8–22 in the examples). Inflate predicted peaks by adding a convex adjustment (bigger add-on when forecast is already above its recent mean).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after regime change: beyond ~20–30 days the model can remain too high when the realized series starts reverting/lowering (e.g., realized drops to ~68–67 while forecast stays ~70–73). Introduce mean-reversion pull toward the last observed spot for horizons >~20 days.",
    "Horizon-dependent calibration: use a piecewise correction—(i) days 1–5: minimal adjustment; (ii) days 6–20: upward bias/trend correction; (iii) days 21–30: damp forecasts toward spot/long-run mean to avoid overextension."
  ]
}
